1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:14,970
If the properties of human language make it such a unique communication system, quite different from the communication systems of other creatures, then it would seem extremely unlikely that other creatures would be able to understand it.
如果说人类语言的种种特性使之成为如此独特的交流系统，与其他生物的交流系统截然不同，那么其他生物似乎就极不可能听懂人类语言。

2
00:00:15,770 --> 00:00:19,630
Some humans, however, do not behave as if this is the case.
然而，从有些人的举动来看，他们并不认为情况如此。

3
00:00:20,430 --> 00:00:30,450
There is, after all, a lot of spoken language directed by humans to animals, apparently under the impression that the animal follows what is being said.
毕竟，人们会对动物说很多话，看样子是以为动物能听懂他们在说什么。

4
00:00:31,250 --> 00:00:33,700
Riders can say “Whoa” to horses and they stop.
骑手对马喊一声“吁”，马就会停下来。

5
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:39,560
Should we treat these examples as evidence that non-humans can understand human language?
我们应该把这些例子当作非人类生物能听懂人类语言的证据吗？

6
00:00:40,360 --> 00:00:41,360
Probably not.
大概不应该。

7
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:53,600
The standard explanation is that the animal produces a particular behavior in response to a particular sound-stimulus or noise, but does not actually “understand” what the words in the noise mean.
通行的解释是：动物针对某种特定的声音刺激或声响做出某种特定的行为，但并不真正“理解”这些声响中的词语是什么意思。

8
00:00:54,400 --> 00:01:06,520
In an early attempt to teach a chimpanzee to use human language, in the 1930s, two scientists (Luella and Winthrop Kellogg) raised an infant chimpanzee together with their baby son.
20世纪30年代，两位科学家（卢埃拉·凯洛格和温思罗普·凯洛格）把一只幼小的黑猩猩和自己尚在襁褓中的儿子放在一起抚养，这是早期教黑猩猩使用人类语言的一次尝试。

9
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:15,190
The chimpanzee, called Gua, was reported to be able to understand about a hundred words, but did not “say” any of them.
据报道，这只名叫古娃的黑猩猩能听懂大约一百个单词，但一个也没有“说”出来过。

10
00:01:15,990 --> 00:01:27,060
In the 1940s, a chimpanzee named Viki was reared by another scientist couple (Catherine and Keith Hayes) in their own home, exactly as if she were a human child.
20世纪40年代，另一对科学家夫妇（凯瑟琳·海斯和基思·海斯）在自己家中抚养了一只名叫维基的黑猩猩，完全把她当成人类的孩子来养。

11
00:01:27,860 --> 00:01:35,470
These foster parents spent five years attempting to get Viki to “say” English words by trying to shape her mouth as she produced sounds.
这对养父母花了五年时间，试图让维基“说”出英语单词，办法是在她发声时设法调整她的口型。

12
00:01:36,270 --> 00:01:42,960
Viki eventually managed to produce some words, rather poorly articulated versions of “mama”, “papa” and “cup”.
维基最终总算说出了几个词，即发音相当含糊的“mama”（妈妈）、“papa”（爸爸）和“cup”（杯子）。

13
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:56,520
In retrospect, this was a remarkable achievement since it has become clear that non-human primates do not actually have a physically structured vocal tract which is suitable for articulating the sounds used in speech.
回过头来看，这是一项了不起的成就，因为现在已经清楚，非人类灵长类动物的声道，其生理构造实际上并不适合发出言语所用的声音。

14
00:01:57,320 --> 00:02:11,930
Recognizing that a chimpanzee was a poor candidate for spoken language learning, another scientist couple (Beatrix and Allen Gardner) set out to teach a female chimpanzee called Washoe to use a version of American Sign Language.
认识到黑猩猩并非学习口语的合适对象后，另一对科学家夫妇（比阿特丽克斯·加德纳和艾伦·加德纳）着手教一只名叫瓦肖的雌性黑猩猩使用美国手语的一个变体。

15
00:02:12,730 --> 00:02:21,740
This sign language has all the essential properties of human language and is learned by many congenitally deaf children as their natural first language.
这种手语具备人类语言的一切基本特性，许多先天失聪的儿童把它当作自己天然的第一语言来学习。

16
00:02:22,540 --> 00:02:30,840
From the beginning, the Gardners and their research assistants raised Washoe like a human child in a comfortable domestic environment.
从一开始，加德纳夫妇和他们的研究助理就在舒适的家庭环境中，像对待人类孩子一样抚养瓦肖。

17
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:37,170
Sign language was always used when Washoe was around and she was encouraged to use signs.
只要瓦肖在场，大家就总是使用手语，还鼓励她使用手势。

18
00:02:37,970 --> 00:02:43,190
In a period of three and a half years, Washoe came to use signs for more than a hundred words.
在三年半的时间里，瓦肖逐渐学会使用代表一百多个单词的手势。

19
00:02:43,990 --> 00:02:53,430
Even more impressive was Washoe’s ability to take these forms and combine them to produce “sentences” of the type “gimme tickle”, “more fruit” and “open food drink”.
更令人惊叹的是瓦肖的这种能力：她能把这些形式组合起来，构成“给我挠痒痒”“还要水果”“打开食物喝”这类“句子”。

20
00:02:54,230 --> 00:03:08,430
Some of the forms appear to have been inventions by Washoe, as in her novel sign for “bib” and in the combination “water bird” (referring to a swan), which would seem to indicate that her communication system had the potential for productivity.
有些形式看来是瓦肖自己的发明，比如她表示“围嘴”的新颖手势，以及“水鸟”这一组合（指天鹅）；这似乎表明，她的交流系统具有能产性的潜力。

21
00:03:09,230 --> 00:03:21,430
At the same time as Washoe was learning sign language, another chimpanzee named Sarah was being taught (by Ann and David Premack) to use a set of plastic shapes for the purpose of communicating with humans.
就在瓦肖学习手语的同时，安·普雷马克和戴维·普雷马克正在教另一只名叫萨拉的黑猩猩使用一套塑料图形，以便与人类交流。

22
00:03:22,230 --> 00:03:27,380
These plastic shapes represented “words” that could be arranged in sequence to build “sentences”.
这些塑料图形代表“单词”，可以按顺序排列起来组成“句子”。

23
00:03:28,180 --> 00:03:31,560
The basic approach was quite different from that of the Gardners.
普雷马克夫妇的基本方法与加德纳夫妇的截然不同。

24
00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:36,930
Sarah was systematically trained to associate these shapes with objects or actions.
萨拉接受了系统的训练，学习把这些图形与物体或动作联系起来。

25
00:03:37,730 --> 00:03:43,450
She remained an animal in a cage, being trained with food rewards to manipulate a set of symbols.
她始终是一只关在笼子里的动物，人们用食物作奖励来训练她操作一套符号。

26
00:03:44,250 --> 00:03:55,190
Once she had learned to use a large number of these plastic shapes, Sarah was capable of getting an apple by selecting the correct plastic shape (a blue triangle) from a large array.
学会使用大量这类塑料图形之后，萨拉就能从一大排图形中选出正确的那个（一个蓝色三角形），从而得到一个苹果。

27
00:03:55,990 --> 00:04:09,040
Sarah was also capable of producing “sentences” such as “Mary give chocolate Sarah” and had the impressive capacity to understand complex structures such as “If Sarah put red on green, Mary give Sarah chocolate”.
萨拉还能造出“玛丽给巧克力萨拉”这样的“句子”，并且具备令人惊叹的能力，能理解“如果萨拉放红在绿上，玛丽给萨拉巧克力”之类的复杂结构。

28
00:04:09,840 --> 00:04:23,890
A psychologist, Herbert Terrace, argued that chimpanzees simply produce signs in response to the demands of people and tend to repeat signs those people use, yet they are treated as if they are taking part in a “conversation”.
心理学家赫伯特·特勒斯认为，黑猩猩只不过是应人们的要求打出手势，而且往往重复那些人所用的手势，然而人们却把它们当作是在参与一场“对话”。

29
00:04:24,690 --> 00:04:34,200
As in many critical studies of animal learning, the chimpanzees’ behavior is viewed as a type of conditioned response to cues provided by human trainers.
正如在许多批判性的动物学习研究中那样，这些黑猩猩的行为被视为对人类训练员所给提示的一种条件反应。

30
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:40,680
Important lessons have been learned from attempts to teach chimpanzees how to use forms of language.
人们尝试教黑猩猩如何使用各种形式的语言，已经从中汲取了重要的经验教训。

31
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:43,150
We have answered some questions.
我们已经回答了一些问题。

32
00:04:43,950 --> 00:04:52,510
Were Washoe and Sarah capable of taking part in interaction with humans by using a symbol system chosen by humans and not chimpanzees?
瓦肖和萨拉能否借助一套由人类而非黑猩猩选定的符号系统，参与同人类的互动？

33
00:04:53,310 --> 00:04:54,920
The answer is clearly “Yes”.
答案显然是“能”。

34
00:04:55,720 --> 00:05:01,500
Could Washoe and Sarah go on to perform linguistically on a level comparable to a two-year-old child?
瓦肖和萨拉能否进而在语言方面表现出与两岁儿童相当的水平？

35
00:05:02,300 --> 00:05:04,270
The answer is just as clearly “No”.
答案同样显然是“不能”。

36
00:05:05,070 --> 00:05:17,280
In arriving at these answers, we have also had to face the fact that, even with our list of key properties, we still don’t seem to have a non-controversial definition of what counts as “using language”.
在得出这些答案的过程中，我们也不得不面对一个事实：即便有了我们列出的那份关键特性清单，对于怎样才算“使用语言”，我们似乎仍然没有一个无争议的定义。

37
00:05:18,080 --> 00:05:23,950
It has to be fair to say that, in both cases, we observe the participants “using language”.
这样说肯定是公允的：在这两种情况下，我们都观察到参与者在“使用语言”。

38
00:05:24,750 --> 00:05:26,250
However, there is a difference.
然而，二者是有区别的。

39
00:05:27,050 --> 00:05:44,650
Underlying the two-year-old’s communicative activity is the capacity to develop a highly complex system of sounds and structures, plus a set of computational procedures, which will allow the child to produce extended discourse containing a potentially infinite number of novel utterances.
在两岁儿童的交际活动背后，是一种能力：能够发展出一套高度复杂的语音和结构系统，外加一套运算程序；这将使孩子得以产出长篇语篇，其中包含数量可能无限的新颖话语。

40
00:05:45,450 --> 00:05:49,060
No other creature has been observed “using language” in this sense.
人们还没有观察到其他任何生物在这个意义上“使用语言”。

41
00:05:49,860 --> 00:05:55,580
It is in this more fundamental or abstract sense that we say that language is uniquely human.
正是在这种更根本或更抽象的意义上，我们才说语言是人类独有的。
